Subject: run-test262 owns the error-file name it was given with -e

What this does
  - The `-e FILE` option copies the file name (`strdup`) and releases a name set earlier by the configuration, instead of keeping a pointer into `argv`.

Why we need it
  `main` ends with `free(error_filename)`. A name from the configuration is allocated, but the one from `-e` pointed into `argv`, so the final `free` released a pointer `malloc` never returned.

Failure
  `run-test262 -e FILE ...` ended in SIGABRT (exit 134) after printing its results, on macOS (`pointer being freed was not allocated`), in every build, and the result line was the last thing a script saw.

Soundness
  - Only the process-exit path of the test runner changes; a name taken from the configuration is freed once, as before.

--- a/run-test262.c
+++ b/run-test262.c
@@ -2225 +2225,2 @@
-            error_filename = get_opt_arg(arg, argv[optind++]);
+            free(error_filename);
+            error_filename = strdup(get_opt_arg(arg, argv[optind++]));
